    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-64907",
    ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y,z)=xyz-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,0,\\pi ).$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =yz\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =yz\\cdot 1-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =yz-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}yz-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)) & =yz-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz) \\\\  & =yz-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)\\cdot (z) \\\\  & =yz-z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz) \\\\  & =z(y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,0,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,0,\\pi )} & =\\pi (0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1\\cdot \\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi (0-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
