    ":question/math-academy-id": "q-70022",
    ":question/problem": "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)=4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{e}^{2y}{z}^{4}.$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{e}^{2y}{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{3}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}{z}^{4}) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-{z}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-{z}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2y}) & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot 2y-{z}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\cdot 2y-{z}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{2y}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}y-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=8{x}^{3}y-2{z}^{4}{e}^{2y}.\n$$"
  },
