    ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{z}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,1,2).$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1,2)} & ={2}^{3}-2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\  & =8-2(1)(-1) \\\\  & =8+2 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
