    ":question/problem": "For the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(4,1,1).$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-x{z}^{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y-x{z}^{4}) & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4y)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{4})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (0-{z}^{4}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (-{z}^{4})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-{z}^{4}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(4,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(4,1,1)} & =-(1)^{4}{e}^{4(1)-4(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  },
