    ":question/id": "a87ec839-cad9-515d-b29b-91381158b553",
    ":question/problem": "For the function $h(x,y,z)=z\\sqrt{4x^2+y^2},$ evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(-2,3,-1).$",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Differentiate with respect to $x$, holding $y$ and $z$ fixed. The factor $z$ stays constant, and the square root requires the chain rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}&=z\\cdot\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4x^2+y^2}}\\cdot\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(4x^2+y^2)\\\\\n&=\\frac{z}{2\\sqrt{4x^2+y^2}}(8x+0)\\\\\n&=\\frac{4xz}{\\sqrt{4x^2+y^2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow substitute $x=-2$, $y=3$, and $z=-1$. The radicand is $25>0$, so this derivative is defined at the point.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}\\right|_{(-2,3,-1)}&=\\frac{4(-2)(-1)}{\\sqrt{4(-2)^2+3^2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{25}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{8}{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"
  }
